    <!-- SIX TRAPS -->
    <section class="section dark" id="traps">
      <div class="inner content">
        <div class="eyebrow">The proprietary IP</div>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="color: var(--white);">Six structural patterns. Not founder failures.</h2>
        <p class="section-sub">
          The traps are cognitive patterns no founder alone, however sharp or diligent, can counteract
          while operating the business they created. The Diagnostic surfaces them so the next-call
          conversation happens on math rather than instinct. Most companies sitting on the
          growth-versus-runway call are running three or four simultaneously.
        </p>

        <div class="traps-grid">
          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-index">Trap 01 · Sunk Cost</div>
            <h3 class="trap-name">The money already spent feels like it is voting.</h3>
            <p class="trap-line">It is not.</p>
            <div class="trap-evidence">
              <div class="trap-evidence-label">Where it shows up</div>
              <ul class="trap-evidence-list">
                <li><span class="tag">Cost</span>Negative-margin product lines still receiving headcount.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Pricing</span>Legacy tiers preserved because "we built the model around them."</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Growth</span>Channels funded on what they produced 12 to 18 months ago.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="trap-fix">
              <div class="trap-fix-label">Unlock move · Marginal analysis framing</div>
              <p>Reframe "what we have invested" to "what the next dollar buys us." Delivered as an explicit pre-mortem on the Keep path inside the Three-Path Model.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-index">Trap 02 · Status Quo</div>
            <h3 class="trap-name">Doing nothing feels neutral.</h3>
            <p class="trap-line">It is never neutral.</p>
            <div class="trap-evidence">
              <div class="trap-evidence-label">Where it shows up</div>
              <ul class="trap-evidence-list">
                <li><span class="tag">Cost</span>Over 40% of recurring costs auto-renewing without re-approval.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Pricing</span>List prices set at launch, untouched through input-cost shifts.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Growth</span>Last year's channel split, still running, no reallocation mechanism.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="trap-fix">
              <div class="trap-fix-label">Unlock move · Default flip</div>
              <p>Monthly forward-defense on every commitment over $5K via the Forward Capital Register. Decisions move from "running by default" to "running by active defense."</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-index">Trap 03 · Mental Accounting</div>
            <h3 class="trap-name">The right total can hide the wrong allocation.</h3>
            <p class="trap-line">A healthy P&amp;L can carry an unhealthy mix.</p>
            <div class="trap-evidence">
              <div class="trap-evidence-label">Where it shows up</div>
              <ul class="trap-evidence-list">
                <li><span class="tag">Cost</span>No unit economics by product. Cross-subsidies invisible.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Pricing</span>Self-serve anchor used in enterprise. One segment funding another.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Growth</span>Marketing as one line. No channel-level CM. No segment-level CAC.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="trap-fix">
              <div class="trap-fix-label">Unlock move · Isolation</div>
              <p>Product Line P&amp;Ls with full cost allocation. Segment-level contribution margin. Channel-level CAC. The cross-subsidy map is the artifact that breaks this trap.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-index">Trap 04 · Anchoring</div>
            <h3 class="trap-name">The first number set becomes the orbit.</h3>
            <p class="trap-line">Every later number gets pulled toward it.</p>
            <div class="trap-evidence">
              <div class="trap-evidence-label">Where it shows up</div>
              <ul class="trap-evidence-list">
                <li><span class="tag">Cost</span>Burn anchored to post-raise plan, not current economics.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Pricing</span>Enterprise pricing anchored to self-serve ARPU. Valuation anchored to last round.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Growth</span>CAC targets anchored to pre-market-turn benchmarks.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="trap-fix">
              <div class="trap-fix-label">Unlock move · First-principles rebuild</div>
              <p>Quarterly model rebuild from current economics. Pricing Architecture Revisit re-grounds pricing from current win-rate and willingness-to-pay data, not the launch anchor.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-index">Trap 05 · Loss Aversion</div>
            <h3 class="trap-name">The pain of admitting a loss is roughly twice the value of recovering the capital.</h3>
            <p class="trap-line">Kahneman's coefficient. It applies to founders too.</p>
            <div class="trap-evidence">
              <div class="trap-evidence-label">Where it shows up</div>
              <ul class="trap-evidence-list">
                <li><span class="tag">Pattern</span>Economically clear cut, six-plus months overdue.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Voice</span>"We can't let them go." "We built this."</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Effect</span>Capital stays committed past the point the math supports.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="trap-fix">
              <div class="trap-fix-label">Unlock move · Three-Path explicit</div>
              <p>Keep / Kill / Restructure on identical metrics. The CEO stops comparing "keep" to "loss" and starts comparing "keep" to "choose."</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-index">Trap 06 · Certainty Illusion</div>
            <h3 class="trap-name">Polished reporting can feel like evidence when it is theater.</h3>
            <p class="trap-line">Confidence intervals do not survive a real downside case.</p>
            <div class="trap-evidence">
              <div class="trap-evidence-label">Where it shows up</div>
              <ul class="trap-evidence-list">
                <li><span class="tag">Pattern</span>Runway as a single number. No downside case.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">Plan</span>Growth plan asserted with no active experiments testing key assumptions.</li>
                <li><span class="tag">History</span>Planning fallacy visible in prior forecast accuracy.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="trap-fix">
              <div class="trap-fix-label">Unlock move · Scenario + experiment + rolling check</div>
              <p>Three-scenario forecast plus Growth Experiment Register plus weekly actuals-vs-prediction. Uncertainty named. Assumptions listed. Experiments testing the most uncertain ones.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- THREE-PATH MODEL -->
    <section class="section alt">
      <div class="inner content">
        <div class="eyebrow">The decision-level instrument</div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Every material decision modeled three ways.</h2>
        <p class="section-sub">
          A single recommendation is a point of view. Three modeled paths is a decision framework.
          Each Three-Path carries its own trap stack, a dollarized unlock move, a named owner, and a
          review date. Five to ten produced per Resolution.
        </p>

        <div class="path-grid">
          <div class="path-card">
            <div class="path-tag">Keep</div>
            <p>The current path, priced truthfully. Full cost structure, opportunity cost, and the trap
            holding it in place. If the Keep column does not hurt to look at, the decision is not
            actually trapped.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="path-card featured">
            <div class="path-tag">Restructure</div>
            <p>The same problem re-sequenced. New pricing, new allocation, new scope, new ownership.
            Where most material decisions land, because most stuck decisions are directionally
            correct and structurally wrong.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="path-card">
            <div class="path-tag">Kill</div>
            <p>Full exit. Dollars returned. Opportunity cost recovered. Narrative cost named. Kill is
            on the page whether or not it is the answer, because a decision that cannot be killed
            has not actually been modeled.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="path-caption">
          The Three-Path Register is the operating artifact. Five to ten Three-Paths is what a
          Resolution engagement produces in 90 days. Every entry includes the Six Trap stack
          (the two or three traps keeping the current path running) and the unlock move.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

